WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.475
The café owner opens her app's home screen.

2
00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:07.604
Some mornings it appears at once.

3
00:00:07.604 --> 00:00:10.379
Most mornings, it hangs for a whole second.

4
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:11.896
Here's the odd part.

5
00:00:11.896 --> 00:00:18.163
The screen is built from 100 small answers, and each one is slow only one time in a hundred.

6
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:21.019
So why is the screen slow most of the time?

7
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:24.659
Let's leave the app and go to the café's kitchen.

8
00:00:24.659 --> 00:00:27.945
A big order comes in: a tray of 100 sandwiches.

9
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.831
A hundred cooks make one each, all at once.

10
00:00:30.831 --> 00:00:33.779
The tray goes out only when every slot is full.

11
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:35.337
Each sandwich is quick.

12
00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:39.792
But one time in a hundred, a cook drops it on the floor and starts again.

13
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.748
Now the whole tray waits for that one cook.

14
00:00:42.748 --> 00:00:45.880
Ninety-nine slots are full, and nobody can leave.

15
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:49.019
So each cook is late one time in a hundred.

16
00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:50.894
How often is the tray late?

17
00:00:50.894 --> 00:00:52.005
Pause and guess.

18
00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:59.539
Most people say about one in a hundred, since that's what each cook does.

19
00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:01.604
Let's send out ten trays and watch.

20
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.939
Six of the ten were late.

21
00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:10.454
Well...

22
00:01:10.454 --> 00:01:12.515
that's not one in a hundred.

23
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:17.919
The tray is on time only if all 100 cooks are.

24
00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:22.861
Each one is on time 99 times in 100, so 0.99.

25
00:01:22.861 --> 00:01:29.121
All 100: 0.99 multiplied by itself 100 times, about 0.37.

26
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.860
So the tray is late 63 times in 100.

27
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.481
Every extra cook is one more chance to draw the dropped sandwich.

28
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.699
The app works the same way, in thousandths of a second, called milliseconds.

29
00:01:41.699 --> 00:01:49.001
To draw the home screen, the program asks 100 places for a piece each, all at once, and waits for every answer.

30
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.720
Each place, a small program or a piece of a big database, is called a leaf.

31
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.915
The screen is the tray, and the leaves are the cooks.

32
00:01:57.133 --> 00:01:59.681
A leaf answers in 5 to 15 milliseconds.

33
00:01:59.681 --> 00:02:04.058
But one time in a hundred, it hits a pause and takes a full second.

34
00:02:04.058 --> 00:02:06.540
And the screen waits the whole second.

35
00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:11.199
When one request sets off many requests behind it, that's called fan-out.

36
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:17.103
The rare slow answers are called the tail, because they sit at the slow end when you sort the times.

37
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:22.907
Fan-out turns the tail into the normal case: 63 screens in 100 take a second.

38
00:02:22.907 --> 00:02:28.513
That's called tail latency amplification, where latency is the time you wait.

39
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.127
So, fix the leaves?

40
00:02:30.127 --> 00:02:36.660
To keep only 1 screen in 100 slow, each leaf could be slow only once in 10,000 answers.

41
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.664
And some pauses can't be removed at all.

42
00:02:39.833 --> 00:02:42.933
Wouldn't it be nice to stop waiting for the unlucky one?

43
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:46.032
Back in the kitchen, the answer is plain.

44
00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:55.031
If a slot is still empty after longer than a sandwich usually takes, ask a second cook to make the same one, and use whichever is ready first.

45
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.741
And the spare sandwich?

46
00:02:56.741 --> 00:02:57.880
The staff eat it.

47
00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.833
So the cost is a little extra work, only for the late ones.

48
00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:08.205
In the app, the second cook is a replica: another computer that holds a copy of the same data.

49
00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:12.728
The program waits 15 milliseconds, longer than any quick leaf takes.

50
00:03:12.728 --> 00:03:17.718
Then it sends the same request to the replica, and keeps the first answer back.

51
00:03:17.718 --> 00:03:19.865
How long does the screen take now?

52
00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:20.876
Pause and guess.

53
00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:25.167
23 milliseconds.

54
00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:29.157
The replica answered while the first leaf was still stuck.

55
00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:32.992
That second copy is called a hedged request.

56
00:03:32.992 --> 00:03:41.561
Over many screens, 99 in 100 now finish within 30 milliseconds, and only 1 request in 100 needed a copy.

57
00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:49.837
A common rule is to wait as long as 95 answers in 100 take, so at most about 5 in 100 get a copy.

58
00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:51.688
A hedge needs two things.

59
00:03:51.688 --> 00:03:54.930
First, each copy must be slow for its own reasons.

60
00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.729
If both cooks share one toaster and it jams, the second cook is stuck too.

61
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:06.364
In a program, that's one faulty new version on every replica, or one overloaded disk under both.

62
00:04:06.364 --> 00:04:08.788
Then the extra request buys nothing.

63
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.127
Second, the request must be safe to run twice.

64
00:04:12.127 --> 00:04:13.827
A spare sandwich is fine.

65
00:04:13.827 --> 00:04:16.615
A second charge on someone's card is not.

66
00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:19.340
Reading a balance twice is harmless.

67
00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:26.511
A card payment is safe only with an idempotency key, a unique label the bank checks so it charges once.

68
00:04:26.511 --> 00:04:29.227
Safe to run twice is called idempotent.

69
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.440
So why was the screen slow most of the time?

70
00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:39.696
It waits for the slowest of 100 answers, and with 100 tries, the rare slow one turns up most of the time.

71
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.124
You can't make every leaf perfect.

72
00:04:42.124 --> 00:04:47.487
So stop waiting for the unlucky one: ask a second copy, and keep the first answer.

73
00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:49.200
One quick check.

74
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.751
Say the screen asked only 10 leaves.

75
00:04:51.751 --> 00:04:55.152
Slow more often, or less, and roughly how often?

76
00:04:55.152 --> 00:04:56.285
Pause and think.

77
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.832
Less often: about 1 screen in 10.

78
00:05:01.832 --> 00:05:05.295
Fewer leaves, fewer chances to draw the slow one.
